WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.420
Today we will look at the determinant of a
matrix:

2
00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:08.190
how it is computed and what its properties
are.

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:16.940
First, let us consider the notion of a
determinant. Every square matrix A of order n

4
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.944
is assigned a number called its determinant.

5
00:00:19.944 --> 00:00:23.700
In Latin, "determinant" means "to limit, to
determine".

6
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:31.860
It is denoted |A| ("the modulus of A"), det A,
or by the Greek letter Δ (delta).

7
00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:40.570
For any square matrix there is a general rule
for computing its determinant,

8
00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:47.950
and in practice special convenient rules are
used to compute determinants.

9
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.041
The way a determinant is computed depends on
the

10
00:00:53.041 --> 00:00:56.080
size of the matrix. Let us look more closely.

11
00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:02.620
A first-order determinant is simply the entry
a₁₁ of the matrix. For example,

12
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.760
let a 1×1 matrix be given.

13
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:09.000
Then the determinant of this matrix equals
eight.

14
00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.252
The determinant of a second-order matrix,

15
00:01:14.252 --> 00:01:17.720
or a second-order determinant, is the number

16
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.340
found by the following formula.

17
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:26.620
Here, from the product of the main-diagonal
entries a₁₁ and a₂₂

18
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:35.820
we subtract the product of the
secondary-diagonal entries a₂₁ and a₁₂.

19
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:42.750
For example, take a 2×2 matrix. The entries of
its main diagonal

20
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:49.490
are minus two and five, and the entries of the
secondary diagonal are one and eight.

21
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:56.040
To find the determinant, we multiply the
entries of the main and secondary diagonals

22
00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:02.340
and subtract the second product from the
first. As a result,

23
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.300
the determinant equals minus eighteen.

24
00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:14.100
The determinant of a third-order matrix, or a
third-order determinant, is the number

25
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.630
computed by this formula. The right-hand side
of

26
00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:20.940
the formula is an algebraic sum of six terms,

27
00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:27.560
each of which is a product of three entries
located in different rows

28
00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.560
and different columns of the matrix.

29
00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:42.500
Let us show this in a diagram. This diagram is
called the triangle rule. First

30
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:48.180
we add the products of the entries on the main
diagonal, then

31
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.149
we subtract the products of the entries on the
secondary diagonals.

32
00:02:51.149 --> 00:02:54.250
Note that the products of entries off the main
and secondary diagonals

33
00:02:54.250 --> 00:03:00.570
form triangles; hence the name of the rule.

34
00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:05.718
Let us find the value of a determinant by the
triangle

35
00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:08.910
rule. First we multiply the main-diagonal
entries:

36
00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:15.910
one, zero and five. Then, following the
triangle rule,

37
00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.380
we form the products of the other entries:

38
00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.850
two, one, two, and minus one, four, three.

39
00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:26.038
A plus sign is placed between these products.

40
00:03:26.038 --> 00:03:29.510
Then, from the entries on the secondary
diagonal,

41
00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:32.586
we form the product two, zero and three.

42
00:03:32.586 --> 00:03:36.430
From the remaining entries, following the
diagram,

43
00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:43.170
we form the products minus one, two, five, and
four, one, one.

44
00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:49.770
A minus sign is placed between them.
Computing, we get the answer: minus two.

45
00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:56.150
Let us look at the main properties of
determinants.

46
00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:59.140
Property one: if the rows of a determinant

47
00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:04.460
are replaced by the corresponding columns, its
value does not change.

48
00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:13.260
Property two: if two rows or two columns are
swapped, the sign of the determinant

49
00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.420
changes to the opposite.

50
00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:21.533
Property three: if the corresponding entries

51
00:04:21.533 --> 00:04:24.790
of any two rows (columns) of a determinant

52
00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:29.530
are equal, the determinant equals zero.

53
00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:38.120
Property four: a common factor of the entries
of any row (column) of a determinant

54
00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.100
can be taken outside the determinant.

55
00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:45.243
Property five: if the corresponding entries

56
00:04:45.243 --> 00:04:48.450
of any two rows (columns) of a determinant

57
00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:54.070
are proportional, the determinant equals zero.

58
00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:59.598
Property six: if the entries of one row
(column) of

59
00:04:59.598 --> 00:05:02.950
a determinant are multiplied by the same
number

60
00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:06.052
and added to the corresponding entries of
another

61
00:05:06.052 --> 00:05:08.710
row (column), the value of the determinant

62
00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:12.286
does not change. Let us look at this property
in

63
00:05:12.286 --> 00:05:15.490
an example. Take a second-order determinant

64
00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:19.256
equal to four. Now we multiply the entries of
the second

65
00:05:19.256 --> 00:05:22.350
row by three and add them to the corresponding

66
00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:26.428
entries of the first row, and then compute the
resulting

67
00:05:26.428 --> 00:05:30.360
determinant. As you can see, the answers are
the same.

68
00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:37.360
To look at the last property of determinants,
let us introduce two new notions.

69
00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.897
The minor of an entry aᵢⱼ of a determinant of

70
00:05:40.897 --> 00:05:44.120
order n is the determinant of order n − 1

71
00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:49.488
obtained from the original one by crossing out
the

72
00:05:49.488 --> 00:05:54.320
row and column that meet at the chosen entry.

73
00:05:54.320 --> 00:06:00.200
It is denoted Mᵢⱼ. Let us look at an example.

74
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.348
Let a third-order determinant be given.

75
00:06:03.348 --> 00:06:07.060
We need to compute the minor of its entry a₁₂.

76
00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:11.200
We cross out the first row and the second
column.

77
00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:17.720
We get a second-order determinant made up of
the entries left after crossing out.

78
00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:20.820
Its value equals minus four.

79
00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:25.865
The cofactor of an entry aᵢⱼ of a determinant
is its

80
00:06:25.865 --> 00:06:29.120
minor taken with a plus sign if the sum of i
and j

81
00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:35.540
is even, and with a minus sign if this sum is
odd.

82
00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:42.240
It is denoted Aᵢⱼ. For example,

83
00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:48.680
in a third-order determinant we need to find
the cofactor of the entry a₁₂.

84
00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:55.180
Then, by the definition of a cofactor, the
cofactor of a₁₂

85
00:06:55.180 --> 00:07:01.920
equals minus one to an odd power times the
corresponding minor M₁₂.

86
00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:09.580
As a result, we take the second-order
determinant with a minus sign and get four.

87
00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:17.690
Now that we know what a cofactor is, let us
look at the last property, number seven.

88
00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:24.690
The sum of the products of the entries of any
row (column) and their corresponding

89
00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:31.200
cofactors equals the value of the determinant.

90
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:36.346
Let us look at this property in an example.

91
00:07:36.346 --> 00:07:42.570
Take a determinant. As the row we use the
first one.

92
00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:48.760
We multiply the entries of the first row by
their cofactors.

93
00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:54.960
Computing the cofactors and doing the
remaining operations, we get the number three.

94
00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:04.650
To sum up: in this lesson we learned what a
determinant is, how it is computed

95
00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:12.270
depending on its order, and how its properties
help to compute determinants quickly.
